/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A position on the earth is a latitude (angle north or south of the equator) and a longitude (angle east or west of the prime meridian), both in degrees measured on an ellipsoid, a smooth mathematical surface close to the real earth.
   - Degrees do not give flat-plane distance.
   - Cylindrical projections wrap a cylinder around the earth; conic projections use a cone; azimuthal projections use a plane touching at one point.
   - UTM divides the world into 60 zones, each 6° of longitude wide, numbered from zone 1 at 180° W.
   - Software reprojects on the fly, converting a layer into the display projection.
*/
